WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.120
Ectoderm can become skin or nervous system.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.360
What decides which one it becomes?

3
00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:10.553
Start with the map.

4
00:00:11.473 --> 00:00:14.853
A blastula is the early frog embryo, a hollow ball of cells.

5
00:00:15.883 --> 00:00:18.983
Inside is a fluid-filled cavity, the blastocoel.

6
00:00:19.743 --> 00:00:22.243
The roof of that cavity is the animal cap.

7
00:00:23.217 --> 00:00:25.817
The cap is ectoderm, the outer germ layer.

8
00:00:26.737 --> 00:00:29.577
It gives rise to both the skin and the nervous system.

9
00:00:31.417 --> 00:00:34.057
The outer layer of skin is the epidermis.

10
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:37.017
Keep that word; it is one of the two answers.

11
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.120
Gastrulation is the set of cell movements that form the germ layers.

12
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.120
It begins at the blastopore, a groove on the lower half of the embryo, where cells

13
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.180
start to move inside.

14
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.730
The tissue at the blastopore's dorsal lip is the organizer.

15
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:58.233
Dorsal is the side where the back and the neural plate will form.

16
00:00:59.153 --> 00:01:00.453
Ventral is the belly side.

17
00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:07.087
In the 1920s, Hilde Mangold and Hans Spemann grafted an organizer into another

18
00:01:07.087 --> 00:01:08.087
embryo.

19
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:10.850
The host formed a second body axis.

20
00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:14.830
The organizer could change what nearby ectoderm became.

21
00:01:15.883 --> 00:01:20.603
For decades, the question was what positive signal the organizer sends to make

22
00:01:20.603 --> 00:01:21.603
ectoderm neural.

23
00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:27.683
Now the dish. Cut out the animal cap and culture it alone in salt solution.

24
00:01:28.733 --> 00:01:31.413
Left alone, what should a piece of skin-to-be become?

25
00:01:32.393 --> 00:01:33.393
Make a guess.

26
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:40.453
It becomes epidermis.

27
00:01:41.213 --> 00:01:43.393
No organizer tissue is attached to it.

28
00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:48.183
One more pair of names.

29
00:01:49.123 --> 00:01:55.143
BMPs, bone morphogenetic proteins, are secreted signals of the TGF-beta family.

30
00:01:56.183 --> 00:02:01.943
Inside a cell, their signal is carried to the nucleus by Smad1, Smad5 and Smad8.

31
00:02:02.917 --> 00:02:04.537
The map raises three puzzles.

32
00:02:05.617 --> 00:02:07.497
First, what holds an intact cap as skin?

33
00:02:08.517 --> 00:02:12.937
Second, what does the organizer send, and how does it change the ectoderm's fate?

34
00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:18.947
Third, does the answer hold outside the frog cap: in the whole embryo, in a fly, in

35
00:02:18.947 --> 00:02:19.947
a chick?

36
00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:24.087
Here is the idea to carry through every chapter.

37
00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:31.167
BMP says skin. In early frog animal caps, turn its signal down far enough, and the

38
00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:35.467
cells become neural, as long as they can still respond and their other needed

39
00:02:35.467 --> 00:02:38.287
inputs, such as FGF, are intact.

40
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:44.280
BMP's effect comes down to one dial, Smad1 activity in the nucleus.

41
00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.520
The next chapters show three hands that turn it down.
